= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**True.** Put $\alpha=\sup A$, $\beta=\sup B$ and $u=\max\{\alpha,\beta\}$. These <suprema> exist by <completeness of the real numbers>, since the <sets> are nonempty and bounded. Every <set element> of the <union> $A\cup B$ is at most $u$, so $u$ is an <upper bound>. Conversely, every <upper bound> of $A\cup B$ bounds both $A$ and $B$, and is therefore at least both <suprema>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sup(A\cup B)=\max\{\sup A,\sup B\}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**True.** Write $\alpha=\sup A$ and $\beta=\inf B$. Every $a\in A$, $b\in B$ satisfies $a-b\leq\alpha-\beta$, giving an <upper bound> for the <set> $A-B$ of pairwise differences. For every $\varepsilon>0$, the definitions of <supremum> and <infimum> give $a\in A$ and $b\in B$ with
$$
a>\alpha-\varepsilon/2,\qquad b<\beta+\varepsilon/2.
$$
Then $a-b>\alpha-\beta-\varepsilon$. Hence no smaller <upper bound> works, and
$$
\boxed{\sup(A-B)=\sup A-\inf B.}
$$
Here $A-B$ means pairwise subtraction, rather than <set difference>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**False.** Take the finite <sets> $A=B=\{-2,-1\}$. Their <suprema> are both $-1$, whereas the <set> of pairwise products is $A\cdot B=\{1,2,4\}$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\sup(A\cdot B)=4\ne1=(\sup A)(\sup B).}
$$
Negative factors can reverse an <inequality>, which prevents the proposed argument with separate <suprema>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For the first <sequence>, $|a_n|=n^{-3}\to0$. The <squeeze theorem> gives $a_n\to0$, and every <subsequence> of a <convergent sequence> has the same <limit of a sequence>. Thus its <set> of <subsequential limits> is $\boxed{\{0\}}$.

For the second <sequence>, the four possible <sine> values give
$$
b_n=\begin{cases}
1,&n\equiv0,2\pmod4,\\
n,&n\equiv1\pmod4,\\
1/n,&n\equiv3\pmod4.
\end{cases}
$$
The even-indexed <subsequence> is constantly one, and $b_{4k+3}\to0$, so both zero and one occur as <subsequential limits>. A <convergent subsequence> is bounded, so it contains only finitely many terms with $n\equiv1\pmod4$. Of the remaining three residue classes, at least one occurs infinitely often. The corresponding further <subsequence> has <limit of a sequence> zero or one; the <subsequence> rule forces the original <convergent subsequence> to have that same <limit of a sequence>. Therefore the exact answer is $\boxed{\{0,1\}}$. Divergence to $+\infty$ along $n\equiv1\pmod4$ is not a finite <subsequential limit>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

The <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> states that every <bounded sequence> of <real numbers> has a <convergent subsequence>. It is enough to select infinitely many terms of the <tangent> <sequence> that stay uniformly bounded.

For each positive <integer> $k$, choose $n_k=\lfloor k\pi+1/2\rfloor$, using the <floor function>. Then $|n_k-k\pi|\leq1/2$. Moreover $n_{k+1}-n_k\geq\pi-1>0$, so these are strictly increasing indices. By the period and <monotonicity> of the <tangent> on $(-\pi/2,\pi/2)$,
$$
|\tan n_k|=|\tan(n_k-k\pi)|\leq\tan(1/2).
$$
Thus $(\tan n_k)$ is a <bounded subsequence>. Applying the <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> to it gives a <convergent subsequence>, which is also a <subsequence> of the original <sequence>. **Such a <convergent subsequence> therefore exists.**

