= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

If $L<1$, set $\varepsilon=(1-L)/2>0$. The definition of <limit of a sequence> gives $a_n<L+\varepsilon=(1+L)/2<1$ for all sufficiently large $n$, contradicting $a_n\geq1$. Hence $\boxed{L\geq1}$.

Factor the difference of cubes. Since both <cube roots> are at least one,
$$
\left|a_n^{1/3}-L^{1/3}\right|
=\frac{|a_n-L|}{a_n^{2/3}+a_n^{1/3}L^{1/3}+L^{2/3}}
\leq\frac{|a_n-L|}{3}.
$$
Given $\varepsilon>0$, the definition of <limit of a sequence> supplies $N$ with $|a_n-L|<3\varepsilon$ for $n\geq N$. The displayed <inequality> then gives $|a_n^{1/3}-L^{1/3}|<\varepsilon$, proving directly that $\boxed{a_n^{1/3}\to L^{1/3}}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $\varepsilon>0$. By the definition of <Cauchy sequence>, choose an <integer> $K\geq1$ such that $|x_j-x_k|<\varepsilon/2$ whenever $j,k\geq K$. Apply the given <inequality> with $N=K+1$. It supplies $n>K+1$ for which
$$
0<x_n\leq\frac12\min_{k<K+1}x_k\leq\frac{x_K}{2}.
$$
Both $n$ and $K$ are in the <Cauchy sequence> estimate, so
$$
\frac{x_K}{2}\leq x_K-x_n<\frac\varepsilon2,
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad x_n<\frac\varepsilon2.
$$
For every $m\geq K$ the <triangle inequality> gives
$$
0<x_m\leq|x_m-x_n|+x_n<\varepsilon.
$$
This is the definition of <convergence> to zero: $\boxed{x_m\to0}$. The proof uses only the <Cauchy sequence> estimate, positivity, and the stipulated <inequality>; it does not assume the general <completeness of the real numbers> theorem for <Cauchy sequences>. Taking $N=K+1\geq2$ also avoids the empty initial <set> in a literal reading of the condition for very small $N$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Put $u_n=11^n\log n/(2^{2n+1}n!)$ for $n\geq2$. The <factorial> gives
$$
\frac{u_{n+1}}{u_n}=\frac{11}{4(n+1)}\frac{\log(n+1)}{\log n}.
$$
The <logarithm> identity
$$
\frac{\log(n+1)}{\log n}=1+\frac{\log(1+1/n)}{\log n}\longrightarrow1
$$
follows from <continuity> of the <logarithm> at one and $\log n\to+\infty$. The product rule for <limits of sequences> therefore makes the displayed ratio tend to zero. In particular, some $N$ satisfies $0<u_{n+1}\leq u_n/2$ for all $n\geq N$. By <mathematical induction>, $0<u_{N+j}\leq u_N2^{-j}$. A <geometric sequence> with ratio in $(0,1)$ tends to zero, so the <squeeze theorem> proves
$$
\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{11^n\log n}{2^{2n+1}n!}=0.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

**The <sequence> does not converge.** For $n=2^k$, the <largest prime factor> is $p(n)=2$, while the <sum of prime factors with multiplicity> is $s(n)=2k$. This <subsequence> therefore has ratio $1/k\to0$.

There are infinitely many <prime numbers>: if there were only $q_1,\ldots,q_r$, a <prime factor> of $q_1\cdots q_r+1$ would be absent from the list. Listing the <prime numbers> in increasing order gives a <subsequence> of indices $n=q_j$ for which $p(n)=s(n)=q_j$, so the ratio is constantly one. Every <subsequence> of a <convergent sequence> has the same <limit of a sequence>. The two distinct <subsequential limits> $\boxed{0\text{ and }1}$ rule out <convergence>.

