= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

A <series> $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n$ **converges** if its <partial sums> $s_N=\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_n$ have a finite <limit of a sequence> $S$ as $N\to\infty$; its sum is then $S$. It has **<absolute convergence>** if the <series> $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|a_n|$ converges, where $|a_n|$ is the <absolute value> of the term.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Assume first that the original nonnegative <series> converges to $S$. Its <partial sums> $s_m$ increase to $S$. Write $t_N=\sum_{n=1}^{N}a_{g(n)}$ for the <partial sums> of the <rearrangement of a series>. Since $g$ is an <injection>, the indices $g(1),\ldots,g(N)$ are distinct, so
$$
0\leq t_N\leq s_{\max\{g(1),\ldots,g(N)\}}\leq S.
$$
Thus $(t_N)$ is an increasing <bounded sequence>, and the <bounded monotone sequence theorem> gives $t_N\to T\leq S$.

For any fixed $m$, <surjectivity> of $g$ gives
$$
N_m=\max\{g^{-1}(1),\ldots,g^{-1}(m)\}.
$$
For $N\geq N_m$, nonnegativity implies $t_N\geq s_m$. Taking <limits of sequences> first in $N$ and then in $m$ gives $T\geq S$. Hence $T=S$.

Conversely, $g^{-1}$ is a <bijection>, and the original <series> is the <rearrangement of a series> of $\sum b_n$ defined by $g^{-1}$. The same argument proves its <convergence> whenever $\sum b_n$ converges. **Both <series> converge together, and their sums are equal.**

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

In level $n$, each group is
$$
2^{-(n+1)}+2^{-(n+1)}-2^{-n}=0,
$$
and there are $2^n$ such groups. The <partial sums> at completed groups are therefore zero. Inside a group of level $n$, the only other <partial sums> are $2^{-(n+1)}$ and $2^{-n}$. Every level contains finitely many terms, so the level containing the $N$th term tends to infinity with $N$. Thus all <partial sums> lie between zero and a bound tending to zero. By the <squeeze theorem>, the <series> has sum $\boxed{0}$.

For the next two parts, label the positive occurrences of size $2^{-(n+1)}$ by $P_n$ and the negative occurrences of size $-2^{-n}$ by $N_n$. There are exactly $2^{n+1}$ occurrences in $P_n$ and $2^n$ in $N_n$. Occurrences with equal value retain separate labels, so checking their counts verifies a <bijection> of terms.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Place the single term in $N_0$ first. For $n=0,1,2,\ldots$ in order, append $2^{n+1}$ copies of the pair
$$
\left(2^{-(n+1)},\ -2^{-(n+1)}\right).
$$
This uses all of $P_n$ and all of $N_{n+1}$ exactly once, so it is an explicit <rearrangement of a series>. Its beginning is
$$
-1,\quad \frac12,-\frac12,\frac12,-\frac12,\quad
\frac14,-\frac14,\frac14,-\frac14,\frac14,-\frac14,\frac14,-\frac14,\quad\ldots.
$$
Every completed pair leaves the <partial sum> at $-1$. Within a level-$n$ pair the <partial sum> differs from $-1$ by at most $2^{-(n+1)}$. As $n\to\infty$ this tends to zero, proving that the rearranged <series> has sum $\boxed{-1}$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Start with all of $N_0$ and $N_1$, namely $-1,-1/2,-1/2$, whose sum is $-2$. For $n=0,1,2,\ldots$ in order, append $2^{n+1}$ copies of the triple
$$
\left(2^{-(n+1)},\ -2^{-(n+2)},\ -2^{-(n+2)}\right).
$$
This uses all $2^{n+1}$ occurrences of $P_n$ and all $2^{n+2}$ occurrences of $N_{n+2}$, exactly once. Together with the initial terms, every original occurrence is used, establishing a <rearrangement of a series>. Its beginning is
$$
-1,-\frac12,-\frac12,\quad
\frac12,-\frac14,-\frac14,\frac12,-\frac14,-\frac14,\quad\ldots.
$$
Each triple has sum zero. At its intermediate terms the <partial sum> is at most $2^{-(n+1)}$ above $-2$. This bound tends to zero, so the whole <series>, rather than just the <subsequence> of sums at completed triples, has sum $\boxed{-2}$.

