The **<intermediate value theorem>** states that if $f:[u,v]\to\mathbb R$ is <continuous> and $y$ lies between $f(u)$ and $f(v)$, inclusive, then some $c\in[u,v]$ satisfies $f(c)=y$. If $y$ lies strictly between the endpoint values, then $c$ lies in $(u,v)$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $n=1$, choose $x=0$, $y=1$. For $n\geq2$, define the <continuous function>
$$
g(t)=f(t+1/n)-f(t),\qquad 0\leq t\leq1-1/n.
$$
Its values at the grid points have the <telescoping sum>
$$
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}g(k/n)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\bigl(f((k+1)/n)-f(k/n)\bigr)=f(1)-f(0)=0.
$$
If one summand is zero, we already have a zero of $g$. Otherwise the summands cannot all have the same sign, so two of these values have opposite signs. The <intermediate value theorem> between their arguments gives $t\in[0,1-1/n]$ with $g(t)=0$. Set $x=t$, $y=t+1/n$. Then
$$
\boxed{|x-y|=1/n,\qquad f(x)=f(y).}
$$
This is the <horizontal chord of reciprocal-integer length> property; the <telescoping sum> explains why reciprocal <integers> appear.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

An explicit <piecewise linear function> is
$$
\boxed{f(t)=\begin{cases}
-3t,&0\leq t\leq1/3,\\
6t-3,&1/3\leq t\leq2/3,\\
3-3t,&2/3\leq t\leq1.
\end{cases}}
$$
The adjacent formulas agree at $1/3$ and $2/3$, so the <function> is <continuous>, and $f(0)=f(1)=0$. Any pair of points at distance $2/3$ can be written $t,t+2/3$ with $0\leq t\leq1/3$. For every such $t$,
$$
f(t+2/3)-f(t)=\bigl(1-3t\bigr)-(-3t)=1.
$$
Thus their values are never equal. The <horizontal chord of reciprocal-integer length> conclusion does not extend to this distance.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The construction is the partial middle-third construction of the <Cantor function>. After $n$ rounds of assigning middle thirds, every remaining open <interval> has endpoint values
$$
\frac{k}{2^n},\quad\frac{k+1}{2^n}
$$
for some $k=0,\ldots,2^n-1$, and every such consecutive pair occurs. This follows by <mathematical induction>. Initially the endpoint values are $0,1$. An <interval> with those consecutive endpoint values receives the value
$$
\frac12\left(\frac{k}{2^n}+\frac{k+1}{2^n}\right)=\frac{2k+1}{2^{n+1}}
$$
on its closed middle third. Its two remaining open <intervals> therefore have consecutive endpoint values at denominator $2^{n+1}$. All previously assigned endpoint values persist. Hence all even numerators at the next denominator were already present, and all odd numerators have just been assigned. Including the initial values zero and one proves
$$
\boxed{\{k/2^n:0\leq k\leq2^n\}\subseteq f(\mathbb R\setminus C)\quad(n\geq0).}
$$
These are precisely the <dyadic rational> values between zero and one.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

We give the explicit point $\boxed{x=1/4}$. Repeatedly choose the left remaining third and then the right remaining third. After $2m$ choices, the resulting closed <interval> is $[L_m,R_m]$, where
$$
L_m=\sum_{j=1}^m\frac2{3^{2j}}=\frac14(1-9^{-m}),\qquad
R_m=L_m+9^{-m}=\frac14+\frac34\,9^{-m}.
$$
These formulas follow from a finite <geometric series>. They show $L_m<1/4<R_m$, and that both endpoints tend to $1/4$. The point $1/4$ is also strictly inside each intermediate left third: its distance from $L_m$ is one quarter of the current length. It therefore never enters any assigned closed middle third or its endpoints, so $1/4\in C$.

Endpoint values in the <Cantor function> construction obey the same left/right choices, with halving in place of thirding. Thus
$$
f(L_m)=\sum_{j=1}^m\frac1{2^{2j}}=\frac13(1-4^{-m}),\qquad
f(R_m)=f(L_m)+4^{-m}=\frac13+\frac23\,4^{-m}.
$$
All these endpoints are in the domain of the partial <function>: $L_m$ is the right endpoint of a middle third assigned at round $2m$, and $R_m$ is the left endpoint of a middle third assigned at round $2m-1$.

Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose $m$ so that $(2/3)4^{-m}<\varepsilon$, and put $\delta=9^{-m}/4$. If $t\notin C$ and $|t-1/4|<\delta$, then $L_m<t<R_m$. The assumed <monotonicity> gives
$$
\frac13-\frac13\,4^{-m}\leq f(L_m)\leq f(t)\leq f(R_m)\leq\frac13+\frac23\,4^{-m}.
$$
Consequently $|f(t)-1/3|<\varepsilon$. This proves directly, on the stated partial domain,
$$
\boxed{\lim_{\substack{t\to1/4\\t\notin C}}f(t)=\frac13.}
$$
There are defined points arbitrarily close to $1/4$, for example $L_m$ and $R_m$, so the <limit of a function> is not vacuous. No <continuous extension> has been assumed. The residual $C$ here omits the endpoints that the question assigns values to; it is the usual <Cantor set> with those assigned endpoints removed.

